  "claim": {
    "anchor": "Sulphur Bank Mine, south shore of Clear Lake, Lake County, California — Pleistocene hot-spring mercury-sulfur deposit. Active hot-spring system since ~150,000 years ago; mined for mercury 1865-1957 (one of California's largest Hg producers). Now EPA Superfund site CAD980893275. The textbook hot-spring quicksilver-sulfur deposit per White & Roberson 1962 (USGS PP 432-A).",
    "description": "Hot-spring mercury-sulfur deposit at the surface mixing zone where rising H₂S-rich fluid meets atmospheric O₂. Headline minerals: native sulfur (from partial oxidation 2 H₂S + O₂ → 2 S° + 2 H₂O) and cinnabar (HgS, the deposit's mining commodity — Sulphur Bank yielded ~450 tons of mercury 1865-1957). The elemental-sulfur reaction itself produces no acid; the measured pH 2-4 belongs to the source acid-sulfate fluid and to more-complete sulfur oxidation outside this modeled branch. Accessory As-sulfides realgar (orange-red AsS) and orpiment (golden-yellow As₂S₃) deposit in the mixing zone. Supporting silica is modeled as opal when its own low-temperature, pH, and concentration gates are met—not cosmetically relabeled quartz.",
    "expects_species": [
      "native_sulfur",
      "cinnabar",
      "realgar",
      "orpiment",
      "pararealgar",
      "pyrite",
      "marcasite"
    ],
